  "Question": "When simplified, \\(\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{125}\\right)^{-\\frac{2}{3}}\\) becomes:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac1{25}\\)",
    "B": "\\(-\\dfrac1{25}\\)",
    "C": "\\(25\\)",
    "D": "\\(-25\\)",
    "E": "\\(25\\sqrt{-1}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "The denominator \\(3\\) in the exponent tells us to use the real cube root. Since \\((-1/5)^3=-1/125\\), and a negative exponent means taking a reciprocal,\n\\[\n\\left(-\\frac1{125}\\right)^{-2/3}\n=\\left(-\\frac15\\right)^{-2}\n=\\frac{1}{(-1/5)^2}\n=\\boxed{25}.\n\\]\nThe base is nonzero, so the reciprocal is defined. The answer is \\(25\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
